Project: RaftKV — A Distributed Key-Value Store in Java
Architecture Overview
Client → [Node1 (Leader)] ⇄ [Node2 (Follower)] ⇄ [Node3 (Follower)]
              ↓ WAL              ↓ WAL              ↓ WAL
         In-memory Map      In-memory Map      In-memory Map

Each node runs: a Raft state machine (Follower/Candidate/Leader), a persistent log (WAL on disk), an RPC layer (plain HTTP or gRPC), and the actual KV store as the "application" that Raft replicates commands to.

Suggested Tech
Java 17+, plain sockets or gRPC (grpc-java) for inter-node RPC — gRPC looks great on a resume and avoids writing your own wire protocol
Jackson for serializing log entries
JUnit 5 for unit tests, plus a custom test harness for chaos testing
Simple HTTP server (Javalin or Spring Boot minimal) as the client-facing API
RocksDB (optional, later) if you want a real embedded storage engine instead of a flat file — skip this in month 1, mention as "future work"
Week-by-Week Plan

Week 1 — Single node + persistence

LogEntry { term, index, command }
Append-only log file (WAL) + in-memory index for fast lookup
Simple KV state machine: apply(LogEntry) → mutates a ConcurrentHashMap
Crash-recovery: reload log on startup, replay into state machine
Unit tests: write 10k entries, kill process, restart, verify state matches

Week 2 — Leader election

Node states: Follower → Candidate → Leader
ScheduledExecutorService for election timeout (randomized, e.g. 150–300ms) and heartbeats
RequestVote RPC: term comparison, vote granting rules (only one vote per term, log up-to-date check)
Test with 3-node cluster: kill leader, confirm new leader elected within ~1s

Week 3 — Log replication

AppendEntries RPC: leader pushes new entries to followers
Commit index: entry committed once replicated to majority
Handle log conflicts (follower's log diverges from leader's — truncate and overwrite)
Client writes go to leader only; leader redirects/rejects if not leader

Week 4 — Hardening + chaos testing + docs

Snapshotting (compact the log once it passes some size, so restart doesn't replay everything)
Chaos test script: randomly kill/restart nodes, drop network calls (Toxiproxy or a fake network layer you write), verify no data loss and no split-brain
Benchmark: throughput/latency under normal vs. partitioned network
README with architecture diagram, sequence diagrams for election + replication, and a recorded demo (asciinema or GIF)
The specific "hard problems" to be ready to discuss
Election safety — why randomized timeouts prevent split votes
Log matching property — how AppendEntries consistency check prevents divergent logs
Linearizable reads — naive reads from a follower can be stale; you either read-through-leader or implement a "read index" check
Snapshot/log compaction — tradeoff between replay cost and snapshot frequency
Idempotent client requests — what happens if a client retries a write after a leader crash mid-commit
Resume bullet (draft)

Built RaftKV, a fault-tolerant distributed key-value store in Java implementing the Raft consensus protocol (leader election, log replication, snapshotting) with gRPC-based node communication; validated correctness under simulated network partitions and node failures, achieving zero data loss across 500+ chaos-test runs.
